---
source: crates/engram-cli/tests/cli/snapshot/help.rs
expression: "f.run_bare(& [\"query\", \"--help\"])"
---
$ engramdb query --help
exit: 0
--- stdout ---
Query memories (unified ranked / filtered retrieval).

`--mode rank` returns every memory passing the type/tag/criticality filters, sorted by composite score (use for context-aware browsing). `--mode filter` requires a positive relevance signal — at least one of `--query`, `--logical`, `--path`, or `--tags` must be set.

Usage: engramdb query [OPTIONS] --mode <MODE> [QUERY]

Arguments:
  [QUERY]
          Search query text (positional). Alternatively pass `--query`

Options:
      --mode <MODE>
          Retrieval mode. Required
          
          [possible values: rank, filter]

      --query <QUERY>
          Search query text (explicit flag; overrides positional when both set)

  -p, --path <PATH>
          Physical path context (scoring signal)

  -l, --logical <LOGICAL>
          Logical scope context — dot-notation; scoring signal, not a filter. Repeatable

  -t, --type <TYPE>
          Filter by type (repeatable)

      --tags <TAGS>
          Filter by tags (comma-separated or repeated)

      --min-criticality <MIN_CRITICALITY>
          Minimum criticality threshold

  -n, --max-results <MAX_RESULTS>
          Maximum number of results
          
          [default: 10]

      --detail-level <DETAIL_LEVEL>
          Detail level: summary, content, full

      --include-expired
          Include expired memories

      --epistemic <EPISTEMIC>
          Filter by epistemic class: fact, observation, decision (repeatable)

      --situation <SITUATION>
          Your situation, to reweight classes: session_start, file_edit, debugging, design_choice

      --include-invalidated
          Include invalidated memories (closed validity windows)

      --show-scores
          Show relevance scores alongside results

      --include-global
          Also merge global (cross-project) memories into the results.
          
          Runs the same query against the global store and folds its hits into the project results (deduplicated, re-sorted, truncated). Ignored when `--global` is set (already querying the global store). Note: groups this project is subscribed to already fan in automatically; this flag only adds the everyone/global store.

      --global
          Query the global (cross-project) memory store instead of the current project

      --group <GROUP>
          Query a named group store directly (instead of the current project). Subscribed groups already fan into a normal project query; use this to inspect one group's memories in isolation

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - pretty: Human-friendly colored output with formatting
          - json:   JSON output for programmatic parsing
          - plain:  Plain text output without colors

      --json
          Output as JSON

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress non-essential output

  -v, --verbose
          Verbose output

      --no-color
          No colored output

      --dir <DIR>
          Working directory (default: current directory)

      --embedding-backend <EMBEDDING_BACKEND>
          Embedding backend: auto, onnx, or ollama

      --in-process
          Force in-process model loading — never contact the shared embedding daemon. Equivalent to setting ENGRAMDB_IN_PROCESS=1

      --spawn-daemon
          Spawn the shared embedding daemon if it is not already running, then route through it. By default the CLI only uses a daemon that is already running (connect-only)

      --no-maintenance
          Skip the automatic main-worktree maintenance pass (orphan-project cleanup + a quick store health check) for this invocation. Equivalent to setting ENGRAMDB_DISABLE_AUTO_MAINTENANCE=1

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
